WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.570
- Do you have what it takes
to succeed as a consultant?

00:00:03.570 --> 00:00:05.840
In this video, I'm going
to give you an overview.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:08.800
What, from my perspective,
the key skills are

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:11.390
that you will need to succeed in the role

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:14.860
in a large consulting firm
like McKinsey, BCG, or Bain.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:16.500
So, welcome to another coffee break

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.380
here on my channel, "Firm Learning".

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.190
My name is Heinrich. I'm a
former McKinsey consultant.

00:00:21.190 --> 00:00:24.060
And on my channel, I want to
help you to become successful

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.150
in the first years of your career.

00:00:26.150 --> 00:00:28.180
So without further ado,
let's jump into it.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.370
I will now share with
you the top five skills

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:31.650
that, from my experience,

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:33.030
you need to succeed as a consultant.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.670
And I will also share with you,

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.670
for each of these skills, one tip.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.490
What you can do to become more successful,

00:00:39.490 --> 00:00:41.030
more developed in this area.

00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:44.180
The first skill I want to
talk about is business acumen.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.500
And maybe you might already wonder

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.130
whether this is actually a skill

00:00:47.130 --> 00:00:48.370
or whether this isn't something

00:00:48.370 --> 00:00:50.770
that people naturally have, or don't have.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.910
I strongly believe that
business acumen is a learned

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:54.950
and can be developed.

00:00:54.950 --> 00:00:56.260
But first, let's better understand

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.850
what business acumen actually is.

00:00:57.850 --> 00:00:58.683
And for this,

00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:00.617
I will reference the
definition on Wikipedia.

00:01:00.617 --> 00:01:02.500
"Business acumen, or also 'business savvy'

00:01:02.500 --> 00:01:05.750
or 'business sense', is
keenness and quickness

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:08.620
in understanding and dealing
with a 'business situation'

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.310
in a manner that is likely
to lead to a good outcome."

00:01:11.310 --> 00:01:13.250
So this is a bit of a soft term, indeed.

00:01:13.250 --> 00:01:15.960
My perspective for this,
the implicit knowledge,

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:19.230
the implicit understanding
of business situations,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.150
it is a bit of an intuition
that you will form over time.

00:01:22.150 --> 00:01:24.490
What the right decision is for business,

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:25.323
or maybe also,

00:01:25.323 --> 00:01:27.600
what just does not make
sense to follow at all.

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:30.320
And in my experience, an
absolutely necessary condition

00:01:30.320 --> 00:01:32.760
to build business acumen
is an understanding

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.100
of fundamental technical
topics in business.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.035
And this will be especially
things like accounting

00:01:37.035 --> 00:01:38.270
and finance.

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.570
I would argue the most people

00:01:39.570 --> 00:01:42.270
that have a solid understanding
on accounting and finance

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.290
will be able to build this
business acumen quite quickly.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:47.460
But if people do not understand
these concepts at all,

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:49.020
they will really struggle to do so.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.660
But why is business acumen
even needed in consulting?

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.750
But of course you will
facing senior leaders

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:55.270
of the client organization.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:58.105
These people are experienced
business professionals

00:01:58.105 --> 00:02:00.400
and, trust me, that most of these people

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:02.720
surely will have good business acumen.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.790
And of course, if these people
pay significant per diem,

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.010
significant fees for consultants,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.090
they assume that they can talk
to these people at eye level.

00:02:11.090 --> 00:02:14.500
That these people have this
good business sense as well.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:15.333
If you lack this,

00:02:15.333 --> 00:02:17.280
you will completely
undermine your authority.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.580
The client will not take you seriously.

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:22.010
And if you do that, you will
also fail as a consultant.

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:24.040
But now, how can you improve
your business acumen?

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.430
What can you do about it?

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.190
And of course, the best way to do that

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:29.080
is certainly a business education.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.120
Nowadays, there are
lots of online courses.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.630
For instance, there are
many MBA courses available

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:35.780
on online learning platforms like Udemy

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:37.170
where, for a couple of bucks,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.070
you can get access to a
solid foundational course

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:41.150
covering these topics.

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:42.680
The next ability I want to talk to

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:46.060
is the ability to interact
and influence people.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:48.430
Very frequently, especially in consulting,

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:50.100
you will be faced with a situation

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.580
where you want other
people to do something

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:55.340
even though you do not
have direct authority

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:56.173
over these people.

00:02:56.173 --> 00:02:57.440
Indeed, often in consulting,

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.117
you work together in client teams

00:02:59.117 --> 00:03:02.290
where you will be made
responsible for the results

00:03:02.290 --> 00:03:04.030
that this team is producing.

00:03:04.030 --> 00:03:05.990
But of course, as an external person,

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.690
an external service provider,

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.910
you cannot order these
people to do anything.

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:11.370
Indeed, you need to convince them.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:12.790
You need to influence them.

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:14.410
You need to persuade them

00:03:14.410 --> 00:03:16.590
that the things that you
believe are the right things,

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:17.470
that these are the things

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:19.400
that these people want to follow and heed.

00:03:19.400 --> 00:03:22.480
This challenge is also called
leading without authority.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.840
It is quite well studied.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.110
There are lots of thoughts
and ideas around it,

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:27.790
how you can make this work.

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:28.710
The great thing about this

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.990
is that research on this topic agrees

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:33.020
that influence can be learned.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:35.320
It is a skill that you
can develop over time.

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.900
To get you started on this
topic, in my video description

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.280
you'll find a list of
book recommendations.

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:41.510
And there are some books

00:03:41.510 --> 00:03:43.710
that especially focus on this issue.

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:45.960
The first book I would
recommend you to get started

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.960
is "Influence " from Robert Cialdini.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.660
Cialdini is probably the
most renowned researcher

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:53.180
on the topic of influence.

00:03:53.180 --> 00:03:54.370
In his book, he explains

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:57.950
what the most important
tactics are to influence people

00:03:57.950 --> 00:04:00.590
with lots of very practical
examples and insights.

00:04:00.590 --> 00:04:01.960
The next skill that you need to have

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.950
to become successful as a consultant

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:06.260
is quantitative analytical skills.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:08.270
Surely, especially in the first years

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:09.840
of your career as a consultant,

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.380
you will work with data a lot.

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:14.270
You will crunch numbers in
Excel and other programs

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:16.930
and a good, solid,
quantitative understanding

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.320
is definitely important.

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.130
The tool that you will use most frequently

00:04:20.130 --> 00:04:22.380
to crunch the numbers is Excel, indeed.

00:04:22.380 --> 00:04:25.480
And I definitely think it would
be very much worth your time

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.160
to improve your Excel
skills as much as possible

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.810
because strong Excel skills alone

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.840
can carry you in your
first years in consulting.

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.380
That can really make a
difference to your team

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:35.840
and therefore, also for your career.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.760
If you do want to improve
your Excel skills,

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:40.270
I have a whole playlist
on my channel with videos,

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.450
showing you some skills

00:04:41.450 --> 00:04:43.240
that are relevant for
working in consulting.

00:04:43.240 --> 00:04:45.920
I will link this playlist
above here so check that out

00:04:45.920 --> 00:04:47.010
to improve your Excel game.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:48.720
The next important area of skills

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:51.410
that you need to build
are communication skills.

00:04:51.410 --> 00:04:54.200
Of course, communication
is extremely important

00:04:54.200 --> 00:04:55.070
as a consultant.

00:04:55.070 --> 00:04:57.180
And it surely is also heavily interlinked

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.100
with what we talked about influence

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:00.250
a couple of minutes ago.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.083
As a consultant,

00:05:01.083 --> 00:05:04.040
you're frequently in a position
to lead client meetings,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.960
to sit together also with senior people

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.190
in the client organization, talk to them,

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:09.720
convince them of your ideas.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:10.670
And communication skills

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:12.771
are surely extremely important for that.

00:05:12.771 --> 00:05:14.230
While in consulting, another area

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.680
where your communication skills
would be heavily assessed

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:18.710
is how good you are with PowerPoint.

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.810
How good you are with
creating slide presentation,

00:05:21.810 --> 00:05:22.960
slide documents.

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.180
It is not only a cliche

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.110
that as a consultant you
will produce lots of slides.

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.050
For many types of projects, a slide deck

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:30.760
is one of the key deliverables

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.170
that you will leave the
client with after you leave.

00:05:33.170 --> 00:05:36.110
So ensuring that this slide
deck communicates well

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:38.464
and delivers all the key
messages in a great way

00:05:38.464 --> 00:05:40.230
is surely very important.

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:42.190
And if you would like to
improve your skills here,

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:44.940
I once created an end-to-end course

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.520
where I teach you all the skills

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.010
that you need to create
slide presentations

00:05:49.010 --> 00:05:52.590
to the level of leading
consulting firms like BCG or Bain.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.170
I will include a link to that course

00:05:54.170 --> 00:05:55.610
below in the video description.

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:56.500
So if you're serious

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:58.910
about improving your slide writing skills,

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:00.600
I would recommend you to check that out.

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.770
Last but not least is resilience.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.270
I do think that it is extremely important

00:06:05.270 --> 00:06:08.270
that you develop some resilience
techniques for yourself

00:06:08.270 --> 00:06:10.340
because otherwise the
stress and the pressure

00:06:10.340 --> 00:06:11.740
that you will certainly face

00:06:11.740 --> 00:06:13.660
on many of these consulting projects

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.760
will be very difficult for you to handle

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:18.640
if you do not have good
resilience techniques in place.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:19.473
Resilience is a thing

00:06:19.473 --> 00:06:22.270
that probably comes easier to
some people than to others.

00:06:22.270 --> 00:06:23.410
But here, I also believe

00:06:23.410 --> 00:06:26.280
that definitely there are
great ways to improve this

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:27.170
and to work with this.

00:06:27.170 --> 00:06:30.230
In the end, resilience is a
lot about self reflection,

00:06:30.230 --> 00:06:31.730
about personal development,

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:34.400
and setting the right
boundaries when necessary.

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.550
It is just the truth.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.727
There are many consulting projects

00:06:36.727 --> 00:06:39.270
you will be faced with
a significant workload

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.170
and then also additional pressure
imposed on you by clients

00:06:42.170 --> 00:06:43.760
and sometimes, even also your team.

00:06:43.760 --> 00:06:46.240
Being successful in
consulting is a marathon.

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:47.240
It is not a sprint.

00:06:47.240 --> 00:06:48.073
So you need to make sure

00:06:48.073 --> 00:06:50.450
that you do not burn out and have a plan.

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:51.800
Have techniques in place

00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:53.820
that will carry you through
it in the long-term.

00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:56.400
I once created a video where
I talked about techniques

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:59.240
that I use to cope with stress and anxiety

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.510
in my consulting job.

00:07:00.510 --> 00:07:01.890
I will link it somewhere above here.

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:04.090
I hope that there are some
thoughts and reflections in it

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:05.700
that are helpful to you as well.

00:07:05.700 --> 00:07:07.670
So let me summarize the five skills.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:10.760
Business acumen, influence,
analytical abilities,

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.530
communication and resilience.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:14.170
These are definitely important

00:07:14.170 --> 00:07:16.370
for you to become
successful as a consultant.

00:07:16.370 --> 00:07:19.050
And now I would love, of
course, to hear also from you.

00:07:19.050 --> 00:07:21.910
What are the skills that
you felt were most crucial

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:22.790
while you worked as a consultant?

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.470
I will do my best to
answer every single comment

00:07:25.470 --> 00:07:26.303
that you write.

00:07:26.303 --> 00:07:27.870
I would love to get your
input on this as well.

